+### Which jobs carry the flush
+
+`Bc250VmidRingFlushNeeded` (`driver/kmd/vmid_pool.h`) decides for each job whether its frame carries the flush.
+The decision follows amdgpu (Linux v6.18, commit 7d0a66e4). amdgpu flushes the VMID of a job only in these cases:
+
+| Case in amdgpu | Source | Equivalent in this driver |
+|---|---|---|
+| The VM receives a VMID that it does not own | `amdgpu_ids.c`, lines 354 and 433 | `Bc250VmidAdmit` claims a VMID for the job |
+| The `tlb_seq` of the VM increased after the last flush of the VMID. An unmap, a BO move and a PDE move increase it | `amdgpu_ids.c`, line 365 | VidMm sends `FLUSH_TLB`, and the paging buffer invalidates `Bc250VmidFlushMask` (section 2, "FLUSH_TLB") |
+| The CPU writes the page tables of the VM | `amdgpu_ids.c`, line 347 | The `CPU_VIRTUAL` updates write only the tables of the system paging process, which runs at VMID 2 on node 1 |
+| The last flush of the VMID is not emitted yet, or it ran on another ring and did not signal yet | `amdgpu_ids.c`, line 360 | Only the gfx ring runs jobs at the pool VMIDs, and its flushes come before each later job in ring order |
+| A GPU reset occurred | `amdgpu_vm.c`, line 788 | `Bc250VmidResetAll` (teardown, retained resume) and `Bc250VmidRingForget` (soft recovery) |
+
+In all other cases, the next job of the same VM at the same VMID runs without a VM flush. The `FLUSH_TLB` case
+needs one property of the KMD. The KMD does not set `DXGK_GPUMMUCAPS::InvalidTlbEntriesNotCached`, so VidMm also
+sends `FLUSH_TLB` for the VA ranges that change from invalid to valid (`d3dkmddi.h`, WDK 10.0.26100). The GpuMmu
+examples show `FLUSH_TLB` after `UpdatePageTable` (`display/examples.md` in windows-driver-docs). The
+documentation does not say whether `FLUSH_TLB` also follows `COPY_PAGE_TABLE_ENTRIES`, which changes tile mappings
+on the paging companion context (section 5).
+
+The table keeps two fields for each VMID. `RingRoot` is the root of the newest committed ring flush, and
+`RingProcess` is the process of that flush. A job carries the flush when one of these conditions is true:
+
+1. `EnableVmFlushEveryJob` is 1 (section 6).
+2. The job names an explicit VMID. Only the `IB_AT` escape does this. It can change its own page tables without
+   `FLUSH_TLB`, so each of its jobs flushes.
+3. `Bc250VmidAdmit` claimed the VMID for this job.
+4. `RingRoot` is 0, or it is not the root of the job.
+5. The process of the job is unknown, or it is not `RingProcess`.
+
+Condition 4 covers a refused emission. After a claim, a refusal leaves the new root in the table and the old root
+in the register. `RingRoot` changes only after a committed frame without a fault, so it still names the old root.
+The next job of the VMID then writes the root again.
+
+Condition 5 is a second protection. amdgpu compares the VM, not the address of the root. A process can free its
+root page, and VidMm can then put the root of the next process at the same address. The `FLUSH_TLB` for the new
+tables also covers this case.
+
+In the steady state, a game and DWM each keep their VMID and their root. Each VMID flushes once, and the next
+jobs run without a flush. The frame of such a job is the frame of 0.7.216.24, dword for dword.
+
